//? Title: Restoring division: actual comparison-first64-bit register updates
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Unsigned integer division must produce an exact quotient and a remainder below
the nonzero divisor. The canonical restoring producer reads all64 dividend bits,
compares the shifted remainder before subtracting, and records its actual
register states. The original 217/13,100/7 and 1000000/999 examples remain. The
old report-local signed trial loop is removed, so hypothetical add-backs are no
longer counted as executed additions.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

At each actual bit, append the next dividend bit to the current remainder. If
the shifted remainder is at least D, subtract D and set that quotient bit;
otherwise keep the shifted remainder and a zero quotient bit. The prefix
invariant is
$$ N_{prefix}=Q_{prefix}D+R,\qquad 0\le R<D. $$
\alttext(Each processed dividend prefix equals the committed quotient prefix times the divisor plus a bounded remainder.)
Appending a bit preserves the equality before the optional subtraction; the
comparison chooses exactly the new quotient bit needed to restore the remainder
bound. Induction over 64 actual register steps proves the final identity. This
implementation tests before subtracting, so a failed comparison executes no
negative subtraction and no add-back. A trial-subtract hardware variant is
mathematically related but is a different operation history. Fixed-width
arithmetic work is64 bit steps; selected subtraction count depends on the
quotient bits.

